{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310752,"nonce":"5ttURBcWXhfeTZwoX9Qi8hVV9oQ=","title":"wasm long-gen \\\"OOM\\\" was a flash-attn concurrency bug + load peak, NOT a decode leak; wasm frees no general objects","message":"Long-gen wasm \"OOM\" investigation (2026-06-15, branch wasm-inguest-worker-pool). Symptom: `run --wasm` long generation traps with `memory fault at 0x7f000014` inside Tensor_flash_attn_decode. Measured + bisected to ground truth (DON'T trust the OOM framing):\n\n**ROOT CAUSE = a concurrency bug in MY parallel flash attention, not OOM** (commit b195156). The q-head-banded flash shared ONE pre-allocated `scores` Vec across the worker pool (disjoint slices per worker). Disjoint or not, concurrent worker writes into the single buffer corrupted the heap → a float landed in a pointer field → wild `0x7f000014` (≈1.7e38 as f32) read ~330 tokens in. **The fault address is CONSTANT even as memory size changed (1930→1716MB) → it's a wild pointer, NOT running out of memory.** Bisection (the decisive method): inline bands = clean; single worker band = clean; multiple concurrent flash workers = corrupt. Per-worker-scratch (b195156) fixed the TRAP but NOT correctness — the parallel attention was still NON-DETERMINISTIC (greedy run1≠run2 at cache_len\u003e=256; diverged from sequential; degenerated long-gen into repetition loops — that was the \"correctness bug\" the user reported). **ENTIRE flash q-head parallelization REVERTED (8b90d1b).** Root constraint: the pool's worker threads lack proper per-thread TLS, so ANY concurrent guest memory op on a worker is unsafe — worker malloc races (the per-worker scratch), and even disjoint shared-buffer writes corrupt (the shared scratch). **The matmul pool is safe ONLY because its workers are allocation-free pure SIMD with no per-thread state.** To parallelize attention (or anything that allocates/uses runtime state) on the pool, the workers must first run the full wasi thread-start (TLS/thread-id) setup, not just `__stack_pointer`. **Speed recovered WITHOUT threading (c1979f7):** the in-guest flash attention ran SCALAR on wasm (dot_slice_f32/axpy_slice SIMD paths were aarch64/x86-only); added wasm32 +simd128 f32x4 paths → 16→~24 tok/s moderate ctx, ~19 at 1000-ctx, single-threaded + deterministic. So attention threading is NOT needed for now; if revisited, fix worker TLS first. Lesson reinforced twice: run the CONTROL (sequential GUEST_THREADS=1 clean; parallel trapped/diverged) AND verify CORRECTNESS not just no-trap (greedy France MATCH was \u003c256 cache_len = sequential path, so it never exercised the parallel attention).\n\n**Decode does NOT leak.** alloc-track (new feature-gated tracking allocator, commit ebf36c2: `--features alloc-track`, prints net-live high-water ±32MiB to stderr) + the leak-audit workflow both confirm: GQAttention hand-frees every per-token tensor; \u003c32MB net growth over 639 decode tokens. The old \"390KB/tok leak\" is largely gone post string-fix.\n\n**The heap profile (alloc-track):** load climbs to 1898MB (GGUF file buffer 770MB + weights ~889MB + KV cache ~256MB all coexisting), DROPS to 1145MB when the file buffer frees (GGUFLoader.hx:24 bytes.free()→haxe_bytes_free→real dealloc WORKS). wasm memory never shrinks so it stays pinned ~1930MB. Mitigation (commit e254586): KVCache F32 backing now LAZY (first append, after file-buffer free) so cache+file never coexist → peak 1898→1641MB.\n\n**Memory max is ALREADY 4GB** (wasm_runner.rs:5184 `MemoryType::shared(512,65536)` + `--max-memory=4294967296`). NOT the limit. The real \u003e2GB blocker is the **wasi-libc 2³¹ signed sbrk/pointer boundary** (rt_alloc `ptr as i32`); crossing 2GB breaks the allocator. So \"increase linear memory\" needs a u32/allocator fix, not a max bump.\n\n**\"wasm degenerate vs native\" (repetition) — mostly the SAMPLER, not a wasm bug** (581dd46). After the attention-bug revert, wasm is coherent but at temp 0.7 fell into sentence-level repetition loops more than native. Cause: (a) the 1B model repeats anyway (native does too), (b) wasm's f32 reduction order drifts from native (ULP) onto a more loop-prone greedy trajectory, (c) the repetition penalty was OFF for greedy and a weak 1.15/64-window for sampled. Fix = NO-REPEAT-NGRAM (commit 902b436), NOT a stronger penalty. The windowed multiply-penalty is the WRONG tool: weak (1.15) → verbatim BLOCK loops (block \u003e window so the repeat is unpenalized); strong (1.3+/256-window) → GARBAGE coherency AND −20 tok/s native (the per-logit recent scan in topk_scan scales with window). Real fix in LocalTempSampler (llama-chat/Main.hx, final = commit 75471fc): no-repeat-NGRAM with NG_N=8 (array-backed ring prefix + open-addressed hash set of emitted 8-grams); drop top-K candidates that would repeat a seen 8-gram, fall through only if all banned. **CRITICAL: n must be LARGE (8), not 3/4.** At n=3/4 a recurring term's own token sequence (e.g. \"Voronoi\" = 3 tokens) got hard-blocked on repeat → forced a DIFFERENT (mis)spelling each time, deterministically (so temperature didn't fix it — user caught this). n=8 only blocks long verbatim sequences (loops are 150 tokens; terms 2-7) → term spelling untouched (temp 0.7: \"Voronoi\" 15/15 consistent vs ~9 variants at n=3). Greedy topK 1→8 (escape candidates). Windowed multiply-penalty default 1.0 (OFF — even mild 1.1 demotes term tokens; strong loops→garbage). ~50 O(1) hash probes/token; native 77 tok/s. The penalty IS applied on both backends (native runtime/tensor.rs:1341 + wasm kernels.rs rayzor_tensor_topk_scan_f32 take recent+rep_penalty) — it was just the wrong mechanism.\n\n**KEY FINDING — wasm frees NO general objects** (answers \"how are we certain objects are freed on wasm?\"): wasm_backend.rs:1653 \"The wasm backend emits no frees.\" `rayzor_free`/`_rt_free` are intentional NO-OPs (lib.rs:1430, 117). Native uses Arc-COW + an InsertFree pass; on wasm InsertFree is NOT applied. Only reclaimed on wasm: (1) explicit `.free()` tensors/bytes → real dealloc, (2) a conservative subset of transient strings (`compute_string_free_points`). Anon structs / closures / boxed values / escaping strings LEAK — survivable today only because the decode hot path is tensor-dominated + allocates few small objects/token. Real \"wasm GC\" = make InsertFree's Arc-COW drops emit real frees on wasm (risky: needs correct refcount semantics, currently no-op to avoid UAF). Open, not yet done.\n\nRelated: [[perf_wasm_inguest_worker_pool]] (the pool itself), [[bugs_wasm_long_gen_string_oom]] (prior 2³¹ framing — the boundary is real but this trap was the concurrency bug, not the heap).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_wasm_oom_and_object_freeing` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}